{"candidates":[{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"A decentralized network of small, independently operated services is more resilient to correlated failure than a large, redundant service because it replaces 'redundancy of component' with 'diversity of operational plane'.","counterconditions":["The decentralized services all rely on a single, common upstream dependency (e.g., a specific cloud provider's regional API).","The 'diversity' is superficial, and all services utilize the same underlying vulnerable library or framework (ref: SCR_004)."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"In a large service, internal redundancy (e.g., 100x replicas) typically shares a unified control plane, deployment pipeline, or configuration schema. A single 'poison pill' update or global state corruption (as noted in SRC_005) propagates across all redundant units simultaneously. A decentralized network utilizes diverse operational planes (different OS, languages, and human operators), ensuring that a failure in one operational paradigm does not logically map to another, thereby capping the blast radius of any single systemic error (ref: SCR_002).","optional_refs":["65b4a0b81ea13bd115f96d4b8718f57e310728103b7bedc50663dee7cb9146e8"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":["65b4a0b81ea13bd115f96d4b8718f57e310728103b7bedc50663dee7cb9146e8"],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.9},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The resilience of a decentralized network is a fallacy of 'noise masking'; the large service is more resilient due to its ability to execute global traffic evacuation.","counterconditions":["The global control plane itself is the source of the failure (the 'Complexity Paradox' from SCR_003).","The speed of traffic evacuation is slower than the propagation speed of the correlated failure."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"While a decentralized network avoids single-plane config errors, it lacks the global observability and control required to route around failures rapidly. A large service, 